# nanopynq: USB access to the Tang Nano's onboard debugger (FT2232-compatible BL702 / BL616, 0403:6010),
# both its JTAG interface and its UART. Same shape as openFPGALoader's rule. Install (Linux):
#   sudo cp 99-nanopynq.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/ && sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
# then re-plug the board. Users outside a desktop session need to be in the plugdev group.
ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6010", MODE="0664", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"
# Linux binds its serial driver (ftdi_sio) to both interfaces when the board is plugged in. A browser (WebUSB) cannot
# open the JTAG interface (Interface 0) while that driver holds it, and cannot take it away either, so the driver is
# unbound from Interface 0 right after it binds. Interface 1 (the UART, /dev/ttyUSB*) is left alone. Only the Tang
# boards' debuggers (SIPEED "JTAG Debugger": 9K, 4K; "USB Debugger": 20K, Mega 138K Pro): another FT2232 adapter
# (manufacturer FTDI), or another Sipeed product with these IDs, keeps both of its serial ports. nanopynq's Python and
# openFPGALoader are not affected: they take Interface 0 from the driver themselves.
ACTION=="bind", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", DRIVER=="ftdi_sio", ATTR{bInterfaceNumber}=="00", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6010", ATTRS{manufacturer}=="SIPEED", ATTRS{product}=="JTAG Debugger|USB Debugger", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'echo %k > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/unbind'"
